-- title:
--   Non-negativity of the maximum norm
-- statement:
--   For every $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$,
--   $$0 \;\le\; \|x\|_\infty,
--   \qquad\text{where}\quad \|x\|_\infty = \sup_i |x_i| .$$
--
--   Non-negativity is one of the norm axioms for $\|\cdot\|_\infty$, and it is the hypothesis most often discharged when applying results that require a non-negative bound — for instance when a cube side length is read off as a maximum norm.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Since $\|\cdot\|_\infty$ is defined as the indexed supremum $\bigsqcup_i |x_i|$, the statement is not purely formal in the degenerate case $d = 0$, where the supremum of the empty family is $0$ by convention; the inequality holds there as an equality.
